CAT = "hs2Law"; SEG = "LAW"
Q = [
("Regulations such as COSHH and RIDDOR are made under which Act?",
 "Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974", ["Factories and Workshops Act 1961", "Workplace (Health, Safety and Welfare) Regulations 1992", "Management of Health and Safety Regulations 1999"],
 "The Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974 is the enabling Act: COSHH, RIDDOR, the Management Regulations and most other workplace regulations are made under it."),
("Under section 2 of the 1974 Act, an employer must protect employees' health, safety and welfare to what standard?",
 "So far as reasonably practicable", ["Only where a law names the risk", "Absolutely, whatever the cost", "Only once an accident has happened"],
 "Section 2 qualifies most employer duties with 'so far as is reasonably practicable', so the employer weighs the risk against the cost and effort of controlling it."),
("What does 'reasonably practicable' ask an employer to do?",
 "Weigh risk against the cost of control", ["Spend only what this year's budget allows", "Remove every hazard at any price", "Copy what similar firms do"],
 "Controls are needed unless the cost, time or trouble is grossly out of proportion to the risk. Cost alone is not an excuse when the risk is significant."),
("A business has 5 or more employees. What must it have in writing under the 1974 Act?",
 "Its health and safety policy", ["Every staff member's medical history", "A contract with a safety consultant", "A copy of the 1974 Act itself"],
 "Section 2(3) requires an employer with five or more employees to write down its health and safety policy and bring it to employees' attention."),
("Section 3 of the 1974 Act extends an employer's duties to which people?",
 "Visitors, contractors and the public", ["Its own employees only, while on site", "Its shareholders and their families", "Only children under 16 on the premises"],
 "Section 3 covers people who are not employees but may be affected by the work, such as visitors, contractors and members of the public."),
("Which is an employee's legal duty under section 7 of the 1974 Act?",
 "Take reasonable care of self and others", ["Pay for their own safety boots", "Write the firm's safety policy", "Carry out the fire risk assessment"],
 "Section 7 makes employees take reasonable care of themselves and of others affected by what they do, and co-operate with the employer on safety."),
("A worker borrows a fire extinguisher to prop open a heavy door. Which duty does this break?",
 "Not to misuse safety equipment", ["To report near misses within a day", "To attend yearly safety training", "To keep their workstation tidy"],
 "Section 8 makes it an offence for anyone to intentionally or recklessly interfere with or misuse anything provided for health and safety."),
("Can an employer charge staff for the safety equipment the law requires them to use?",
 "No, it must be provided free", ["Yes, by deducting it from wages", "Yes, but only half the cost", "Only for replacement items"],
 "Section 9 of the 1974 Act bars an employer from charging employees for anything done or provided to meet a specific legal requirement."),
("Which body usually enforces health and safety law in shops, offices and hotels?",
 "Local authority", ["Health and Safety Executive", "Police service", "Fire and rescue service"],
 "Local authority environmental health officers enforce in most retail, office, catering and leisure premises; HSE covers factories, construction and similar."),
("Which body enforces health and safety law on construction sites and in factories?",
 "Health and Safety Executive", ["Local authority environmental health", "Environment Agency", "Trading Standards service"],
 "HSE inspectors are the enforcing authority for higher-risk sectors such as construction, manufacturing and agriculture."),
("Which of these can an inspector do when visiting a workplace?",
 "Take photographs and samples", ["Fine the business on the spot", "Sack a careless employee", "Close the firm's bank account"],
 "Inspectors can enter premises, examine and investigate, take samples, measurements and photographs, and interview people. Fines are set by a court."),
("A shop's fire exit route has no lighting. The inspector allows time to fix it. Which notice fits?",
 "Improvement notice", ["Prohibition notice", "Emergency closure order", "Formal caution"],
 "An improvement notice sets a deadline to put a breach right. A prohibition notice stops an activity where there is a risk of serious personal injury."),
("What does a prohibition notice do?",
 "Stops a seriously dangerous activity", ["Gives 21 days to fix paperwork", "Sets the size of the fine to pay", "Warns of a future inspection visit"],
 "A prohibition notice stops the activity until the matter is put right. It can take effect immediately."),
("A firm appeals against an improvement notice. What happens to the notice meanwhile?",
 "It is suspended until the appeal", ["Its deadline is doubled in length", "It is cancelled for good", "It turns into a prohibition notice"],
 "An appeal suspends an improvement notice. A prohibition notice stays in force during an appeal unless the tribunal directs otherwise."),
("Where does a business appeal against an improvement or prohibition notice?",
 "An employment tribunal", ["The county court", "The local council", "The Health and Safety Executive"],
 "Appeals against enforcement notices go to an employment tribunal, normally within 21 days of the notice being served."),
("What is the result of ignoring an improvement notice after its deadline?",
 "A criminal offence", ["A verbal warning", "A longer deadline", "A lower insurance premium"],
 "Failing to comply with an enforcement notice is an offence in its own right and can lead to prosecution."),
("Which insurance must most employers hold by law to cover claims from injured staff?",
 "Employers' liability insurance", ["Public liability insurance", "Buildings and contents insurance", "Life insurance for each employee"],
 "The Employers' Liability (Compulsory Insurance) Act 1969 requires it. Public liability insurance covers the public and is not compulsory."),
("How must an employer show employees what health and safety law requires of them?",
 "Show the HSE poster or give the leaflet", ["Read the Act aloud to staff each year", "Email a link once at interview", "Pin up the last inspection report"],
 "The Health and Safety Information for Employees Regulations require the approved law poster to be displayed or the equivalent leaflet to be given to each worker."),
("Under the Management Regulations, who must an employer appoint to help it meet its duties?",
 "A competent person", ["A union official", "A local councillor", "An insurance broker"],
 "Regulation 7 requires one or more competent people, with enough training, experience and knowledge, to help the employer comply."),
("Which of these is a direct, insured cost of a workplace accident?",
 "Compensation on a claim", ["Lost orders while staff are off", "Time spent investigating", "Damage to the firm's reputation"],
 "Insurance usually pays compensation claims, but many costs, such as lost production, investigation time and reputation damage, are uninsured."),
("Which of these is an uninsured cost an accident often brings?",
 "Overtime to cover the absence", ["Damages a court awards to a worker", "Repair of an insured building", "A payout on a liability claim"],
 "Uninsured costs such as overtime, retraining, lost production and sick pay are usually far larger than the insured costs."),
("Which three reasons are usually given for managing health and safety?",
 "Moral, legal and financial", ["Social, political and cultural", "Personal, local and global", "Legal, medical and technical"],
 "Employers have a moral duty not to harm people, a legal duty under the law, and a financial interest in avoiding the costs of accidents."),
("Which body enforces health and safety at work law in Northern Ireland?",
 "HSENI", ["The Health and Safety Executive", "The Scottish Government", "The Home Office"],
 "Northern Ireland has its own law, the Health and Safety at Work (Northern Ireland) Order 1978, enforced by HSENI and district councils."),
("Under the Workplace Regulations, what should a workroom's temperature usually be at least, for light work?",
 "16°C", ["13°C", "18°C", "20°C"],
 "HSE guidance gives a minimum of about 16°C in workrooms, or 13°C where the work involves rigorous physical effort. No maximum is set."),
("Which welfare facility must every workplace provide under the Workplace Regulations?",
 "Drinking water", ["A staff canteen", "A smoking shelter", "Free hot meals"],
 "The Workplace Regulations 1992 require toilets, washing facilities, drinking water and somewhere to rest and eat meals. A canteen is not required."),
("Which duty falls on designers, makers and suppliers of work equipment?",
 "Ensure it is safe when properly used", ["Train every buyer's staff", "Carry out each buyer's risk assessment", "Keep it safe in the buyer's store"],
 "Section 6 of the 1974 Act places duties on those who design, manufacture, import or supply articles for use at work to make them safe when used properly."),
("An organisation's gross failings in managing safety cause a worker's death. Which offence may it face?",
 "Corporate manslaughter", ["Gross misconduct", "Breach of contract terms", "Unfair dismissal"],
 "The Corporate Manslaughter and Corporate Homicide Act 2007 makes an organisation liable where gross failings by senior management cause a death."),
("Who may decide to prosecute a health and safety offence in Scotland?",
 "Procurator Fiscal", ["HSE inspector", "Local sheriff officer", "Employment tribunal"],
 "In Scotland inspectors report cases to the Crown Office and Procurator Fiscal Service, which decides whether to prosecute."),
]
